course: dictionary
lesson: 1768
title: Clepsydra: the ancient water clock
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A clepsydra is a kind of clock, and you can work out its meaning from the clues around it. «The Greeks called the water clock a clepsydra, meaning "water thief", and used it to give each speaker in court the same time.» The words water clock and the phrase giving each speaker the same time are the clues. A clepsydra must be a clock that uses water to measure time.
explain: Check your guess by swapping in your meaning. «The Greeks used a water clock to give each speaker in court the same time.» That still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. The second sentence adds a clue too: «gave every speaker in court the same amount of water, just like a debate-club timer.» A timer tells you when your time is up, and that is what a clepsydra did.
explain: The word itself came from Greek, and the example says it meant "water thief". This is the name's origin, not its everyday meaning. The dictionary meaning is simple: a water clock, an ancient device that measures time by how much water drips into or out of a container. When you meet a word like this, use the context first, then use the dictionary to be sure.
fact: A clepsydra is a water clock.
fact: A clepsydra measures time by how much water drips into or out of a container.
fact: Ancient Egypt, Greece and China all used water clocks before mechanical clocks were invented.
fact: Ancient Athenian courts used a clepsydra to time speeches, so each speaker got the same amount of time.
fact: You may see it written as "a water clock (clepsydra)", which shows that the two words mean the same thing.
exercise: «The Greeks called the water clock a clepsydra, meaning "water thief".» What is a clepsydra? | *A water clock | A type of Greek speech | A thief who steals water | A mechanical clock with gears
exercise: In ancient Athens, what did courts use a clepsydra for? | *To time each speaker | To wash the court floors | To catch thieves | To store wine
exercise: Which kind of clock measures time by water dripping? | Hourglass with sand | Sundial | Pendulum clock | *Clepsydra
test: A clepsydra measures time by how much what does? | *Water drips into or out of a container | Gears turn | Sand falls | The sun shines on it
test: When were clepsydras used? | Only after mechanical clocks were invented | *In ancient times, such as in Egypt, Greece and China | Only in the future | In the 1990s, after phones were invented
exam: Which swap shows that clepsydra means water clock? | Put "a loud speech" into the sentence and it still makes sense | Put "a pet dog" into the sentence and it still makes sense | Put "bread" into the sentence and it still makes sense | *Put "water clock" into the sentence and it still makes sense
